1. Introduction
Most convective heat transfers in both natural and industrial settings involve turbulent flows, where momentum, energy, and other properties are transported by turbulent energy transfer from large-scale to smallscale eddies through a process known as energy-cascading [1]. This process contributes to the chaotic and unpredictable behaviour of turbulent flows [2]. Despite extensive research, our understanding of the physics of turbulent flow, particularly in the presence of rough walls, remains incomplete. Current knowledge is primarily based on statistical and phenomenological theories [3,4], supported by experimental data and high-fidelity numerical simulations. These theories have been employed by various researchers to predict friction drag and heat transfer in wall-bounded turbulent flows. Rough-wall turbulent heat transfer is a common phenomenon across a diverse array of natural processes and engineering applications, where surface roughness can significantly influence costs, emissions, and energy efficiency. This includes roughness from erosion and deposition on gas turbine components and internal combustion engines [5–11], heat transfer during ice accretion on wind turbine blades [12–16] and aircrafts [17–20], roughness on heat exchangers/sinks or thermal management devices fabricated by additive manufacturing [21–26], topological features and vegetations on the Earth’s surface affecting heat exchange between the Earth’s surface and the atmosphere in
meteorology and the Atmospheric Boundary Layer (ABL), and complex terrains in renewable energy [27]. Additionally, enhancing heat transfer, such as improving cooling efficiency, is crucial for the performance of numerous industrial devices [28–30]. Roughness can influence the wettability of surfaces in both natural and engineering systems, leading to complex flow dynamics [31], a subject that remains poorly understood [32]. In microfluidics for thermal management, the infinitesimal length scale of the system makes roughness a critical factor influencing performance [33]. Artificially generated surface roughness, characterised by distinct topological features such as ribs, grooves, pins, and fins, has been widely utilised for passive heat transfer enhancement [34–39]. Despite extensive research, the effectiveness of roughness in achieving substantial heat transfer improvement has yet to be conclusively proven. In practice, roughness involves topographical features ranging from a few micrometres (e.g., from 5 μm up to nearly 40 μm on the surface of metallic additive-manufactured components [22]) to a few millimetres (e.g., approximately 1 mm on gas turbine blades or greater for biofouling on ship hulls) and can be as large as vegetation and tall buildings and mountain ranges in meteorology and the ABL. As schematically shown in Fig. 1, roughness can be classified into 2D and 3D roughness [40,41]. The term 2D roughness refers to surfaces with a linear profile, varying in only one direction, such as spanwise or streamwise ribs and grooves with different cross-sections. This type of
