roughness is artificially designed for heat transfer enhancement in various applications [42]. The term 3D roughness refers to surfaces with areal height maps consisting of regular or irregular roughness elements that are systematically or randomly distributed. Irregular 3D roughness can occur naturally as a consequence of manufacturing processes [43] or mechanisms such as erosion, corrosion, and deposition, as seen in energy systems [5], gas turbines [44], ice accretion on aircraft wings [45], deposit in combustion chambers [46], and biofouling on ship hulls and marine machinery [47]. Both 2D and 3D roughness have been widely employed for improving aerodynamic performance [48] and heat transfer enhancement [49]. Despite significant advancement, rough-wall turbulent flow and heat transfer remain incompletely understood, as highlighted by comprehensive literature reviews [50–52]. The challenge of rough-wall convective heat transfer involves predicting both flow friction (drag) and heat transfer. Currently, no universal predictive method exists due to insufficient understanding and the varying effects of different roughness topologies on flow. The uncertainties arising from this lack of reliable prediction incur costs amounting to billions of dollars annually [51,53]. Consequently, roughness continues to be a dynamic and prolific field of research. While rough-wall momentum transfer has been studied for over a century [52], the first documented research of rough-wall heat transfer was conducted by Cope [54] in 1941, focusing on 2D roughness elements using air. The 1960s saw the development of some theories of rough-wall heat transfer through experimental work, while recent works have benefitted high fidelity simulations as well [4,55–63]. Despite the long history, research findings on heat transfer have lagged significantly behind those on momentum transfer (friction drag), as illustrated in Fig. 2. Chung et al. [51] highlighted that heat transfer remains one of the primary challenges in the study of rough-wall turbulent flows. As evidenced in Fig. 2, research on rough-wall heat transfer has recently become more active, which can be attributed to three major factors. Firstly, the advancement of simulation techniques and the significant rise in computational power have made scale-resolving computational fluid dynamics (CFD) [46,55,60–68], including Direct Numerical Simulation (DNS) and Large Eddy Simulations (LES), more accessible. The numerical methods can visualise the flow dynamics by providing the distribution of the field’s variables, such as velocity and temperature, that is impossible or difficult to achieve with current experimental methods. They also provide high degree of flexibility to systematically study rough-wall flows by altering roughness topology and flow parameters. Secondly, ever-increasing progress in additive manufacturing (3D printing) technologies has created new opportunities for heat transfer applications, such as in gas turbine components [22,26, 69–77], electronic cooling [78–83], and heat sink and heat exchangers
[82,84–89]. Therefore, considering additive manufacturing roughness, which is due to natural consequence of the process, has become of great importance [90–94]. Lastly, growing global concerns about climate change have increased the need for improved heat transfer solutions in renewable energy [37,95–101] and energy efficiency [102], as well as for technologies aimed at reducing the global carbon footprint [103–106]. This has created opportunities for implementing passive heat transfer techniques, such as artificial roughness, and complex designs, including lattice structures, intricate channels, and triply periodic minimal surfaces (TPMS), fabricated through additive manufacturing, which inherently produces natural roughness on the surfaces of these structures. Accurate and reliable correlations for both friction factors and heat transfer coefficients are crucial for engineering calculations. Developing these correlations requires high-quality experimental data, high-fidelity numerical simulations, and in-depth studies to enhance the understanding of flow dynamics. Empirical correlations and models may be integrated into numerical simulations for practical engineering purposes (e.g., the models developed by Aupoix [107–109], Radenac et al. [110], Aupoix & Spalart [111], and Lee [112]). Developing models requires comprehensive understanding of the structure of turbulent flow, as illustrated in Fig. 3, which is the focus of the present research. Several textbooks offer information on flow and heat transfer over surface roughness, including those authored by Schlichting & Gersten [113], Wilcox [114], Lin [115], Kays, Crawford & Weigand [116], and Saha et al. [40]. Previous reviews [49–52,117–119] have mainly concentrated on the effects of roughness on flow dynamics. Jim ́enez [50] reviewed experimental studies on rough-wall turbulent flows, introducing the d-type and k-type roughness classifications. Flack and Schult [117] examined predictive methods and correlations for frictional drag, while Flack and Schultz [120] explored outer-layer similarity. Raupach and Thom [121], Nepf [122], and Finnigan [123] reviewed studies on ABL flows, with a focus on dense roughness. García-Mayoral et al. [118] reviewed engineered surfaces designed for drag reduction. Piomelli [119] assessed numerical studies conducted at Queen’s University’s Turbulence Simulation and Modelling Laboratory in Canada. Chung et al. [51] provided an overview of advances in rough-wall turbulent flows, and Kadivar et al. [52] reviewed the fundamentals and theories underlying these phenomena. Although recent reviews [51,52] have touched on heat transfer to some extent, they have primarily concentrated on momentum transfer and flow drag due to roughness. The first literature survey on the Nusselt number and pressure drop over rough surfaces was conducted by Bhattacharyya [124] in 1964, published by the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA). Subsequently, Bons [6,125,126] reviewed heat transfer and flow friction in gas turbine components, roughened due to degradation. Ligrani [127] provided an overview of various heat transfer augmentation technologies, including roughness, for cooling gas turbine components. Other researchers have focused on passive heat transfer enhancement using artificial 2D and 3D roughness elements [34–39,128]. Soleimani and Eckels [49] explored drag reduction and heat transfer enhancement through riblet roughness in channel flows. Rowin et al. [129] reviewed measurement equipment and methods for accurately predicting heat transfer over rough walls. Hantsis & Piomelli [57] examined recent high-fidelity numerical studies of passive scalar transport over rough walls, with a focus on turbulent flow properties and budgets. To the best of the author’s knowledge, no comprehensive review currently exists that thoroughly addresses the theory and fundamental analysis of rough-wall turbulent heat transfer. This study aims to fill that significant gap by offering a critical review of the experimental, numerical, and theoretical studies on turbulent convective heat transfer over rough surfaces. The review provides an overview of both historical and recent research in this area, examining key theories, the structural aspects (as shown in Fig. 3), and the effectiveness of surface roughness in enhancing heat transfer. This review paper aims to address critical gaps
