• It provides a detailed overview of the state-of-the-art on rough-wall heat transfer and examine the analogy between heat and momentum transfer in turbulent flows, identifying where the current understanding is robust or needs refinement. • This review examines the impact of roughness topological parameters, Prandtl number, and Reynolds number, and how these factors interplay to influence the thermal boundary layer and roughness efficiency. • It critically evaluates various scaling laws and empirical correlations in light of the proposed theories and hypotheses, comparing them with observed data to highlight discrepancies, limitations, and areas that demand further investigation. • Finally it draws informed conclusions, outlines current limitations, and proposes directions for future research.
2. Rough-wall convective flow
In the literature, heat and mass transfer are often treated as analogous scalar quantities, enabling findings from one field to be applicable to the other. For instance, temperature (T) findings can be transferred to mass concentration (c), and heat flux (q) findings to mass flux (G) by substituting molecular diffusivity (α) with mass diffusivity (D ) and
setting the heat capacity (cp) to one. Dimensionless numbers can also be used interchangeably; for example, the Nusselt number (Nu) can be used in place of the Schmidt number (Sc), and the Stanton number (St) can be substituted for the Sherwood number (Sh). Mass transfer is particularly crucial in ABL flows, where interactions with surfaces such as water, land, vegetation, sand, and urban areas involve the transfer of momentum, heat, and mass. This area of study, known as interfacial transport phenomena, plays a significant role in the global balance of gases such as carbon dioxide, methane, nitrous oxides, and dimethyl sulfide [130–145]. Given that much of the existing literature emphasizes heat transfer, this study also prioritizes heat transfer analysis to build on and contribute to the established body of knowledge. Nonetheless, the principles and findings are equally relevant to scalar transport such as mass transfer.
2.1. Hydraulically rough flow
A boundary layer flow is classified as hydraulically rough when the surface roughness elements are large enough to disrupt and influence the smallest turbulent eddies near the wall. According to phenomenological theory [146,147], these smallest eddies are characterised by the
Kolmogorov length scale, η = (ν3/ε)1/4, and the time scale, tη = (ν/ε)1/2, where ν is the kinematic viscosity and ε is the turbulent dissipation rate. The relative size of the roughness length scale (k) compared to η determines the extent to which roughness impacts the flow. If k < η, the roughness is confined within the viscous sublayer (VSL), where viscous forces dominate and effectively dampen any disturbances caused by the roughness, resulting in a hydraulically smooth flow. Conversely, when k > η, the roughness disrupts the energy cascade, generating roughness-scale eddies that enhance turbulent mixing and increase both viscous and form drag. Pioneering studies by Hagen [148] and Darcy [149] established the
