2.3. Wall origin and virtual wall
Since roughness moves the entire flow upward [51,52], the choice of the origin of the wall-normal coordinate is crucial. The wall origin is defined as the level obtained by smoothing out the roughness geometry to create an idealised smooth surface [156]. This new reference level on the virtual wall is referred to as the wall origin and is denoted by d, also known as the wall offset. As a result, the outer turbulent flow perceives its origin at y = d rather than y = 0 (see Fig. 4). The wall offset is an important factor in wall similarity profiles (discussed further in section 9). To resolve the flow-physical inconsistency introduced by roughness, the shifted wall-normal coordinate y d is used. This adjustment ensures that all profiles in the outer layer properly overlap, adhering to the concept of wall similarity. The selection of the zero-plane displacement is further detailed in Chung et al. [51] and Chan-Braun et al. [157]. While the velocity at y = 0 is zero due to the no-slip boundary condition, the velocity at y = d better reflects the actual flow dynamics in the presence of roughness. The wall origin becomes particularly crucial when the roughness represents a significant portion of the boundary layer thickness. Thom [158] and Jackson [156] proposed positioning the wall origin at the centre of the drag profile on the roughness element. This approach involves adjusting the rough-wall logarithmic velocity profiles to eliminate negative velocities [159]. This adjustment is known as the "zero-plane displacement height," an important concept in meteorology and ABL flows [3]. Analogous to Jackson’s drag-centroid (d), the centre of the heat source distribution from the roughness elements, denoted by dθ, can be used for the origin of the temperature profiles [3]. However, determining dθ can be challenging, often resulting in different wall origins for the velocity and temperature fields. As a result, most studies recommend using the velocity wall origin as the reference for the temperature wall origin (i.e. dθ = d). The drag-centroid approach has been criticized for being potentially inappropriate in certain contexts [51,62,157,160–163]. Another widely used method for determining the wall origin involves fitting the law of the wall to the logarithmic velocity profile (see Section 4) [154, 164–166]. This approach is straightforward and effective for high Reynolds number flows, adhering to wall similarity principles. However, for lower Reynolds numbers, the applicability of the logarithmic profile may be limited. An alternative method for determining the wall origin involves using the mean height of the roughness elements k [162,167–169]. According to Kuwata et al. [62,167,170,171] the wall-normal distance can be
