2.6. Thermal boundary conditions
One of the key differences between momentum and heat transfer is the thermal condition of the surface. In the context of differential equations, there are five types of boundary conditions: Dirichlet, Neumann, Robin, Mixed, and Cauchy, with the first two being the most commonly used. In heat transfer, a uniform wall heat flux (iso-flux) boundary condition represents a Neumann-type boundary, while a uniform wall temperature (iso-temperature) boundary condition represents a Dirichlet-type boundary. Mixed boundary conditions combine elements of both. Apart from boundary conditions, volumetric heat generation can occur within the fluid through phenomena such as nuclear or chemical reactions. The type of boundary condition and the presence of volumetric heat generation can significantly impact the heat transfer process. The impact of thermal boundary conditions on smooth-wall flow has been studied extensively (see, for example,[204–206]). Avellaneda et al. [204] reported different behaviours in temperature profiles with changes in boundary conditions. Kawamura et al. [206] stated that the turbulent Prandtl number in the near-wall region is independent of the wall boundary condition, whereas, in the central region, it is significantly influenced by the boundary condition. Flageul et al. [205] observed a strong impact of the thermal boundary condition on temperature variance. Some studies [200,207] have shown that, when a constant temperature is imposed at the wall, heat and momentum
