5.4. Impact of roughness topology on heat transfer
Similar to friction drag, heat transfer is significantly influenced by roughness topology; however, our understanding of this effect remains less developed, primarily due to a lack of systematic studies. Heat transfer is typically greater on the windward surface of roughness elements [61,92,94], where unmixed fluid with high-temperature gradients interacts. In contrast, the leeward surface experiences well-mixed, low-gradient fluid. In ABL flows, a highly turbulent region formed behind leeward face of mountains leads to the formation of a natural “banner clouds” around isolated mountain peaks [289,290]. Fig. 10 illustrates the instantaneous and average flow from the DNS data of Peeters & Sandham [61]. Flow impinges on the windward slope, and recirculation forms in the wake region behind the roughness elements. The strength of the recirculation can increase with increasing ks, resulting in a momentum deficit. Increasing ks also bring more fresh fluid from the bulk flow region into the roughness layer. The fresh fluid typically impinges on a windward slope (Fig. 10, region B), forming a thin thermal boundary layer, extending both in the streamwise direction, influenced by the mean streamwise momentum, and in the spanwise direction, influenced by the recirculation zone and the adverse pressure gradient. It terminates once it comes under the influence of the mean streamwise momentum again (Fig. 10, region C). The roughness density, specifically the frontal solidity λf, controls the flow impingement mechanism on the windward surface [285]. Similar to friction drag, heat transfer indicates a significant increase within the range of 0.1 < λf < 0.3 [55,64]. For λf ≥ 0.3 sheltering effect occurs, which has been extensively studied in the context of friction drag [51, 265,273,278,282,291,292]. The impact of λf and sheltering effect on heat transfer has recently attracted research attention [55,63,64,68,293, 294]. The flow sheltering effect generally decreases drag in dense regimes, as shown by multiple studies reviewed by Chung et al. [51]. Forooghi et al. [55,164] suggested that the sheltering effect similarly impacts the heat transfer coefficient due to the weaker temperature gradients that forms in the low-speed flow within the sheltered wake regions. Kuwata [63] observed an increase in heat transfer with ES, regardless of Ssk, consistent with the trend of friction drag.
