7. Rough-wall heat transfer models
Due to the chaotic nature of turbulence, no unified theory exists to fully describe turbulent flow. As a result, rough-wall heat transfer is typically characterised using empirical and phenomenological models. Empirical models are formulated based on experimental data, which limits their applicability to specific flows, roughness types, and the ranges of Reynolds and Prandtl numbers. In contrast, phenomenological models are grounded in physical observations, scaling laws, and established hypotheses, providing a broader and more conceptual approach. The logarithmic behaviour of a rough-wall thermal boundary layer is
