7.1. Cavity vortex hypothesis
This was proposed by Owen & Thomson [191] in 1962, and it is probably the oldest rough-wall heat transfer model. A similar approach was published by Dipprey & Sabersky [297] in 1963 taken from Dipprey’s PhD thesis [330] in 1961 at the California Institute of Technology. The term “cavity vortex hypothesis” was suggested by Dipprey & Sabersky [297]. This hypothesis employs the observations of turbulent flow structure close to the wall and leverages the analogy between heat and momentum. The cavity vortex (CV) hypothesis assumes a fully rough flow regime, where drag is independent of viscous effects, and the mean flow pattern remains unaffected by viscosity. Another key assumption is that the roughness elements are closely spaced, causing a sharp decrease in shear stress and flow velocity as the flow approaches the roughness peaks. As a result, the mean flow does not penetrate between the roughness elements, treating the roughness as a sublayer of highly sheared flow. Within this sublayer, the streamwise velocity changes by an amount on the order of uτ over a distance of ks. The CV hypothesis relies on the concept of Coherent Flow Structures
(CFS), schematically illustrated in Fig. 11. Modern simulations and experiments have demonstrated that the turbulent structure in shear flow is governed by CFSs. Two basic forms of CFSs include horseshoe and hairpin vortices [331,332]. Horseshoe vortices are counter-rotating vortices around the roughness elements, forming a shape resembling a horseshoe and creating a complex flow structure around the element. Hairpin vortices are elongated, U-shaped vortices that resemble a hairpin in shape and consist of a pair of counter-rotating vortices connected by a curved flow structure. CFSs contain most of the turbulent energy and are typically found in the productive subrange of the flow. These coherent structures are also responsible for the generation of velocity streaks, which manifest as alternating regions of high and low-speed fluid. Streaks play a crucial role in modulating the transport of heat and momentum in the boundary layer. CFSs draw fluid into the valleys and spaces between roughness elements, scour these regions, and then mix the fluid back into the main flow at the level of the roughness peaks. According to the CV hypothesis, this scouring action is considered the primary mechanism for heat transfer at the wall. The horseshoe eddies extend parallel to the wall and move between the roughness elements, facilitating heat transfer between the surface and the mainstream fluid. They acquire vorticity from the streamwise flow as it moves away from the roughness valleys and experience frictional resistance at the solid surface, where heat is transferred via conduction. The heat is then transferred to the fluid near and above the roughness peaks through the convective motion of the horseshoe eddies. The horseshoe eddies influence the fluid flow, with a thickness approximately equal to height of roughness layer. Since the characteristic thickness of the roughness sublayer is ks and the characteristic velocity is uτ, the heat transfer rate across the unit area of the sublayer is described by [297]
