7.3. Further discussion on heat transfer models
Both the CV hypothesis and KB theory were developed based on the assumption of a fully rough flow regime. In this regime, the flow is independent of viscous friction, and the Reynolds number is sufficiently high to sustain a complete logarithmic layer. However, these models are not generally applicable to transitionally rough regimes, where the flow does not fully satisfy these conditions. Moreover, when Pr ≪ 1.0, as in the case of liquid metals, the thermal boundary layer may extend beyond the interfacial height, yi. In such cases, molecular conduction becomes significant within the logarithmic profile, challenging the assumptions underlying the interfacial concept [231,232]. Consequently, these models are not generally applicable to flows with Pr ≪ 1.0. The CV model operates under the assumption of the Reynolds analogy, which presumes negligible molecular diffusion, with heat and mass
