9. Wall similarity hypothesis
Wall similarity is derived from Townsend’s hypothesis [196] and it is an important concept in turbulent flow, widely used to describe turbulent flow over both smooth and rough surfaces. Townsend proposed that near-wall turbulence consists of two regions: the "active" region, responsible for momentum transfer, and the "inactive" region, which does not influence this transfer. Active turbulence is shear-driven and depends on local flow conditions, while inactive turbulence is generated far from the surface and scales with the outer layer’s parameters. The wall similarity hypothesis states that, in turbulent flow over both rough and smooth surfaces, the turbulent motions outside the roughness (or viscous) sublayer become independent of the surface roughness and viscosity at high Reynolds numbers. This suggests that the normalised turbulent stress, relative to wall shear stress, remains consistent beyond the roughness sublayer and collapses to the smooth-wall data. Therefore, roughness effects are limited to the roughness layer and the outer layer remains unaffected by roughness. The wall similarity hypothesis has been supported by numerous DNS and experimental studies on various types of roughness [52,244]. Some researchers have reported exceptions to the wall similarity hypothesis, suggesting that the outer layer may be sensitive to surface conditions [93,240,244,411–413]. Flack et al. [244] and Garg et al. [93] highlighted exceptions to the wall similarity hypothesis and attributed these discrepancies to the use of "strong" roughness in certain studies. In flows with strong roughness, a significant portion of the log-law region is protruded by roughness elements. Flack et al. [244] highlighted exceptions to the wall similarity hypothesis, attributing them to "strong" roughness, where the roughness height is a significant part of the inner layer. In such cases, the roughness effects can extend into the outer layer, disrupting the flow. Jim ́enez [50] supported this, stating that rough-wall flows with δ/k ≤ 40 might affect the outer layer. Flack et al. [244] suggested a similar criterion, δ/ks < 40. They also showed that Reynolds stresses align well in most of the boundary layer, with turbulence variations confined to y < 5ks. While wall similarity for momentum boundary layer is detailly discussed in the literature (see, e.g. Jim ́enez [50], Flack et al. [244], and Kadivar et al. [52]), the wall similarity for the thermal boundary layer has received less attention. According to wall similarity, the defect temperature profile should only be a function of the normal wall distance (y), represented by
