12. Turbulent statistics
Surface roughness has a profound impact on both turbulent velocity and temperature fluctuations, playing a crucial role in the dynamics of scalar transport. In turbulent flows over rough surfaces, the roughness elements disrupt the coherent flow structures (CFSs), such as velocity and temperature streaks, that are typically observed in smooth-wall turbulence. This disruption leads to increased turbulence isotropy and modifies the distribution and intensity of turbulent and scalar fluctuations. Various studies [4,61–63,66,92,94,170,277] have captured these effects even below the roughness crest and have shown that roughness affects both the magnitude and peak location of scalar fluctuations. In this section, we will mainly focus on the first and second-order turbulent statistics of flow and heat transfer to provide key insights into the behaviour of turbulent heat transfer over roughness. Higher-order statistics, such as turbulent budgets and temperature fluctuation budgets, are beyond the scope of this review, as they have been recently covered in another review paper by Hantsis & Piomelli [57]. Intensity of the velocity fluctuations are considered as the lowestorder indicator of the rough walls’ influence on other flow parts. The most important variables, such as streamwise velocity fluctuation pro
files, uʹuʹ+, and turbulent kinetic energy, TKE+, have been extensively studied for different roughness heights, skewness, effective slope, and roughness types within the regime of fully developed turbulent flow. The impact of roughness topology is generally weaker for the wall
normal, vʹvʹ+, and spanwise velocity, wʹwʹ+, fluctuations [62,67,92–94, 166,244,247,249,250]. Irrespective of the nature of the roughness, the peaks of the velocity fluctuations profiles move slightly away from the wall due to the displacement effect of the roughness on the mean flow.
