13. Conclusion
Despite substantial advancements in understanding friction drag, heat transfer over rough walls has received comparatively less attention.
The majority of studies have concentrated on the fully rough regime, while investigations into heat transfer in transitionally rough and hydraulically smooth regimes remain sparse. The study of flow over rough surfaces presents a complex problem characterised by a vast parameter space, surpassing that of momentum transfer. Both roughness topology and the Prandtl number, Pr, significantly influence heat transfer, with the Pr exerting a particularly pronounced effect. However, existing research is limited to a narrow range of roughness types and Pr. This review summarises the progress made in rough-wall heat transfer, highlights the current limitations, and identifies critical gaps in the literature, setting the stage for future investigations. A substantial portion of the literature examines how roughness topology affects convective heat transfer. Most research has focused on the impact of roughness height, while fewer studies have explored the effects of spatial parameters, such as effective slope ES, and frontal solidity, λf, on heat transfer characteristics. Among other roughness parameters, roughness density has a significant influence on heat transfer. Roughness density affects the impingement of flow on the
