trend persists, it could imply that ΔΘ+ might eventually become negative, suggesting a reduction in heat transfer compared to a smooth wall. The current heat transfer models do not account for either trend, indicating a need for a new theory or a revision of the traditional models. The mechanisms governing heat transfer over rough surfaces are not well understood. From the perspective of turbulence theory, roughness
scale eddies increase with roughness height, leading to greater interactions among turbulent scales and enhanced heat transfer. Phenomenologically, roughness that extends into the higher-velocity flow region facilitates the entrainment of fresh fluid from the bulk flow into the roughness layer, thereby enhancing heat transfer. This suggests the importance of roughness thermal conductivity and its impact of energy transfer from solid wall to the fluid. This mechanism has been hypothesized as a significant contributor to heat transfer in rough-wall laminar flows [91,445]. Heat transfer enhancement by roughness is always accompanied by increased flow friction. Most of the literature reports that the Reynold analogy efficiency, RAR/RAS, is less than 1, suggesting that heat transfer enhancement never exceeds the corresponding increase in friction. However, these findings are limited to certain roughness topologies and flows with Prandtl numbers of 0.7 or 1.0. It can be hypothesized that, in the transitionally rough regime, with a sufficiently high Prandtl number, RAR/RAS greater than 1.0 could be achievable. Higher-order statistics of rough-wall heat transfer have received limited attention. Literature findings demonstrate that both velocity and temperature fluctuations, when scaled in inner units, are attenuated by roughness, with velocity fluctuations being more significantly affected than temperature fluctuations. This difference is likely due to variations in the production mechanisms of velocity and temperature fluctuations. It was also found that the mean vertical turbulent heat flux, vʹθʹ+, is
larger than the mean shear stress, vʹuʹ+, for Pr ≈ 1. This suggests that vertical heat flux plays a significant role inside the roughness sublayer and is important for the production of turbulent fluxes. Furthermore, the turbulent Prandtl number, Prt, behaves unpredictably near the rough surface, indicating that it may not be a reliable tool for modelling generalised rough-wall flows.
13.1. Limitations
Some limitations in numerical studies arise from the application of surface boundary conditions, as well as the restricted range of Reynolds and Prandtl numbers considered. The uniform surface temperature boundary condition is the most commonly used due to its convenient implementation with periodic boundary conditions. The mixedboundary condition has also been employed in some studies to simulate the isoflux condition, although this assumes that the wall remains
